1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:09,463
Say your furnace gives out in January, and the quotes land on the kitchen
table: a new furnace, or a heat pump, and the heat pump is the bigger number.

2
00:00:09,467 --> 00:00:13,392
Is that the real price gap, or are
the quotes pricing different jobs?

3
00:00:13,400 --> 00:00:19,050
And once you know the price, is any of the federal heat
pump money you have heard about still there to help?

4
00:00:20,067 --> 00:00:28,992
The channel Atlas AC puts the usual view plainly: "for upfront cost the furnace
is going to be the cheapest the heat pump is going to be the most expensive".

5
00:00:29,000 --> 00:00:38,413
EnergySage says nearly the opposite: "Installing a heat pump often
costs less than simply swapping in a new AC and furnace or boiler".

6
00:00:38,433 --> 00:00:45,233
The difference is what you compare the heat pump with: a furnace
on its own, or a furnace plus the air conditioner beside it.

7
00:00:46,233 --> 00:00:59,496
Start with the furnace alone: in one database of recorded HVAC jobs, traditional fuel-fired heating
systems averaged $3,000 less than heat pump installations, though a heat pump also does the cooling.

8
00:00:59,800 --> 00:01:10,313
Another analysis put the extra cost of replacing a house's existing heater with
a heat pump, rather than with the same kind of heater, at an average of $6,600.

9
00:01:10,333 --> 00:01:12,153
Now count the air conditioner.

10
00:01:12,167 --> 00:01:24,117
One study's median installed costs put a heat pump at $6,185, just $270 below
a gas furnace with central air, and most of their price ranges overlap.

11
00:01:25,133 --> 00:01:40,283
The first database's report points the same way: in its model projects for homes with no cooling, an all-electric heating,
cooling and hot-water upgrade cost more than a gas one, but the gap was almost exactly the cost of adding central air on its own.

12
00:01:40,300 --> 00:01:54,325
A Canadian model disagrees on the upfront price: it found heat pumps cost more upfront than gas heating with air conditioning,
and come out the lowest-cost option for most households anyway, thanks to their efficiency and doubling as the cooling.

13
00:01:54,333 --> 00:02:09,458
Another study adds a catch: heat pumps designed to carry all of a home's heating are often more expensive to install than an
air conditioner plus gas furnace, because bigger heating loads need bigger heat pumps, new wiring and sometimes panel upgrades.

14
00:02:09,467 --> 00:02:20,379
If you only need a new air conditioner, the gap shrinks: that study says wholesale
heat pumps cost only $200 to $500 more than air conditioners of the same size.

15
00:02:20,700 --> 00:02:30,438
A cold-climate report quoting NREL's cost database put an efficient
heat pump just $370 above an equally efficient central air conditioner.

16
00:02:30,467 --> 00:02:38,804
The study from earlier disagrees, though: its installed medians
put the heat pump about 32 percent above central air alone.

17
00:02:38,833 --> 00:02:52,908
And real installs spread widely: in the first database, a heat pump's price depended most on its size and type, the
home's floor area, the efficiency and the climate zone, and ground-source systems cost far more than other types.

18
00:02:52,933 --> 00:03:02,146
Then there is the electrical work: in a field test of cold-climate
heat pump prototypes at 21 sites, 38 percent needed a new panel.

19
00:03:02,467 --> 00:03:14,054
Where electrical upgrades were needed, they alone cost between $3,750
and $8,970 in labour and materials, before the heat pump itself.

20
00:03:14,367 --> 00:03:25,954
That was a small test, and a typical panel upgrade on its own is put at $1,954
in the first database's report, plus the wiring for each new dedicated circuit.

21
00:03:25,967 --> 00:03:38,392
So against a furnace alone, a heat pump costs more; against a new furnace and air conditioner together it can
come out about even, unless it has to be sized up to carry all the heating, and the wiring can add to either.

22
00:03:39,900 --> 00:03:46,175
So what help is left to bring those prices down:
which tax credits and rebates still exist?

23
00:03:47,200 --> 00:03:57,438
In an earlier video, The Thought Provocateur told viewers "technically these heat
pump tax credits based on the bill that was signed are available through 2033".

24
00:03:57,467 --> 00:04:06,167
That credit was real: from 2023 it was worth 30 percent of
the cost of a qualifying heat pump, capped at $2,000 a year.

25
00:04:06,167 --> 00:04:18,829
But it was cut short: the IRS says the credit is not allowed for any property placed in
service after December 31, 2025, so a heat pump installed after that date cannot claim it.

26
00:04:19,833 --> 00:04:28,071
The Rebate Report pointed viewers elsewhere: "the tax credit for
heat pumps expired, but the rebate program for heat pumps survived".

27
00:04:28,100 --> 00:04:35,800
Those federal rebates, though, were aimed at low- to
moderate-income households and run by each state's energy office.

28
00:04:36,100 --> 00:04:47,288
And some states were slow to start: in one state-by-state tracker, Alabama, Alaska and
Arkansas had their applications only conditionally approved, with no expected launch date.

29
00:04:47,300 --> 00:04:55,313
Owen Marsh, in a video on what survived, counted "12 states and
the District of Columbia" with programs launched as of June.

30
00:04:55,333 --> 00:05:08,658
And on May 29, 2026, the Department of Energy changed the rules: the rebate no longer pays
to replace gas or other fossil-fuel equipment, only to upgrade existing electric equipment.

31
00:05:08,967 --> 00:05:16,554
North Carolina's energy office, for one, says its
program had to make the change by September 1, 2026.

32
00:05:17,567 --> 00:05:34,154
Some state and utility programs are generous: in Massachusetts, Mass Save's whole-home rebate is
$8,500, or $16,000 at the income-based level, against an average install of $22,000 in its 2022 data.

33
00:05:34,167 --> 00:05:48,679
But that is one program: in the first database, which predates today's rules, the median
total rebate across its projects was $1,327, about 21 percent of gross project cost.

34
00:05:48,700 --> 00:05:56,013
And a Canadian model found heat pumps there still cost more
upfront despite rebates meant to bring capital costs down.

35
00:05:56,033 --> 00:06:08,946
So for a heat pump installed in 2026 there is no federal tax credit, the federal rebate no longer
covers switching from gas, and where rebates exist, they usually trim the price rather than erase it.

36
00:06:10,467 --> 00:06:17,429
So before you compare quotes, decide what you would otherwise
buy: a furnace alone, or a furnace and an air conditioner.

37
00:06:17,733 --> 00:06:26,921
Ask every installer up front whether your panel and service can carry the heat pump,
and check your state energy office and your utility for rebates before you sign.
